WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
Welcome to fascinating frontiers, Episode 78 from May 23, 2026.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.000
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.020
SpaceX just flew its largest starship yet on a test

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.500
that ended with a controlled splashdown halfway

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:15.240
around the world.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.980
The Starship V3 vehicle lifted off from Texas

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.620
carrying six third-generation Raptor engines

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:22.600
on the upper stage.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.080
One vacuum-optimized engine shut down early

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.060
yet the flight computer extended burn time on the remaining

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.700
five to reach a usable sub-orbital path.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.820
The upper stage executed a banking maneuver during descent

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.700
that previews the profile needed for a future tower

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:39.880
catch at starbase.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.780
Heat shield performance held up well enough

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.200
for a controlled splashdown in the Indian ocean.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.100
The booster stage experienced an uncontrolled hard landing

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.060
in the Gulf of Mexico after separate engine problems.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.800
The chief executive noted that the upper stage reached its

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.560
intended trajectory even after losing one engine early

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:00.220
in the ascent.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.020
Five remaining engines ran longer than plan to compensate,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.600
demonstrating the vehicle's ability to adapt in real time.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.820
The upper stage completed a controlled descent

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.980
and landed in the target zone after traveling thousands

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.380
of kilometers down range.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.340
On-board cameras captured the final approach

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.620
and confirmed the vehicle remained intact through peak heating.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.500
Now, here's something that blew my mind when I looked into it.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.300
If you scaled a typical re-entry speed to everyday distances,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.740
a spacecraft would cross the length of a football field

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.880
in less than a millisecond, while the air in front of it

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.280
is compressed to temperatures above 1600 degrees Celsius.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.500
The shield does not try to absorb that heat.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.720
Instead, it sheds most of it by ionizing the air into a plasma

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.080
that carries energy away from the vehicle.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.200
Starships' tiles must withstand not only the temperature spike,

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.260
but also the mechanical stress of a banking turn

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:59.920
at hypersonic speeds, where even small gaps can let

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.500
superheated gas reach the underlying structure.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.260
Engineers still cannot predict exactly how the tiles will erode

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.040
after dozens of flights, because the interaction

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.800
between material microstructure, plasma chemistry,

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.540
and vibration remains only partly modeled.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.240
The open question is whether any current tile formulation

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.060
can survive the cumulative damage of repeated high-energy entries

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.900
without requiring replacement after every mission.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.900
Researchers are testing beneficial fungi

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.920
that break down Martian regolith

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.400
and improve its ability to support plant growth.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.980
The approach would let future crews grow food locally

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.400
instead of shipping every calorie from Earth.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.760
Early experiments focus on how the fungi interact

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.680
with the mineral heavy soil, and whether they can sustain

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.060
repeated crop cycles.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.400
A single successful demonstration could reduce the mass of supplies

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.520
needed for long-duration surface days.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.140
Follow-up work will examine how the same microbes behave

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.920
under actual Martian radiation and temperature swings.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.780
On a different note, British Olympic grower Helen Glover

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.320
used a parabolic flight to float through a compact exercise

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.620
device designed for future space stations.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.100
The test checked whether rowing motions

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.060
can provide effective resistance training

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.500
when there is no gravity to work against.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.440
Engineers recorded muscle activation and machine stability

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.040
during repeated zero-g intervals.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.980
Data from the session will guide refinements

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.640
before the hardware is considered for longer missions.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.060
The project aims to keep crews healthy on trips

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.880
that last months rather than days.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:38.520
Shifting focus NASA has released a draft solicitation

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.320
for the contract that has run Caltech's

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.220
Jet Propulsion Laboratory for decades.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.840
The agency's language emphasizes performance metrics

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.200
and cost controls that go beyond routine renewal language.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.020
Current work at JPL includes multiple Mars missions

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.480
the Europa Clipper spacecraft and technology development

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.740
for future astrophysics observatories.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.680
Any change in operator would require an extensive transition

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.320
plan to avoid disrupting ongoing projects.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.440
Comments on the draft are due before NASA issues

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.180
the final request for proposals.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.260
Observers can target the Alpine Valley,

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.160
a 130-kilometer long cleft

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.800
cutting across the lunar Alps,

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.300
while the moon is at first quarter phase.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.400
The feature is best seen when sunlight strikes it

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.920
at a low angle, casting long shadows

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.860
that reveal its depth and rils.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.940
Nearby craters and the Monte's Alpe's mountain range

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.600
provide additional detail for modest telescopes.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.280
The same lunar phase also places Jupyter's moons

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.020
in a compact grouping that changes noticeably

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.180
over a single evening.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.120
Sky this week columns list additional events

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:47.160
through the end of the month.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.680
Laika Ying will become the first person from Hong Kong

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.980
to fly on a Chinese space station mission.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.260
She was chosen after a national selection process

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:58.780
that included medical and technical evaluations.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:02.280
The Shenzhou 23-flight is scheduled to dock

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.760
with the Tian Gong station

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.380
and conduct a series of science experiments.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.800
Her selection marks a milestone

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.080
in expanding China's astronaut corps

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.740
beyond the original cohort.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.500
Training will focus on station systems

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.360
and the specific research tasks assigned to the crew.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.520
Four Russian satellites have moved into orbits

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.180
that bring them within close range

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:26.180
of an ICI synthetic aperture radar satellite.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:29.580
The proximity is described as unusual

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.500
for satellites on standard operational missions.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.820
Observers are tracking the objects

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.980
to understand whether the moves represent

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:39.780
routine station keeping or something more deliberate.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.620
No immediate impact on the commercial satellites data collection

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.540
has been reported.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:49.980
A 2.5-hour sequence captured with the Takahashi CN-212

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:53.340
Cassegrain shows Europa, Ganymede and IO crossing

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:54.340
the face of Jupyter.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.480
Each frame combines 600 short exposures

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.300
that were later stacked and processed.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.420
The video reveals the moon's changing positions

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.260
relative to the planet's cloud bands

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.580
over a single night.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.560
Observers can replicate similar sequences

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:11.700
with mid-sized telescopes when Jupiter is well placed.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.800
Recent reports outline ongoing activity

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.920
at the Tian Gong complex, including crew rotations

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:19.440
and experiment handovers.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.740
The station supports a growing set of life science

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.500
and materials research projects.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:27.280
International partners have expressed interest

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.120
in future collaboration opportunities.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.320
Details on the next crude launch remain tied

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:33.720
to the Shenzhou schedule.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.080
A new shared calendar lists upcoming major showers

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.720
and other notable sky events

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:40.620
with automatic time zone conversion.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.920
Users can import the file into Google Calendar, ICAL,

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:45.620
or Outlook.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.820
The creator built the tool after missing a shower

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.340
due to lack of advanced notice.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.200
The calendar will be updated as new events

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.280
are confirmed by professional networks.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.380
Next time, we'll be watching for further updates

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.420
on Starship's post-flight data review

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:02.500
and the next test flight timeline.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.340
That covers today's space and science news.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.940
Share this with a fellow space enthusiast

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.020
if you've found it interesting.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:10.780
I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:11.760
See you tomorrow.

